# PitWall — Cursor Rules

You are an expert engineer building PitWall, a multiview F1 race-watching tool.
Read ARCHITECTURE.md and brand.md before generating significant code.

## Project shape

Monorepo managed with pnpm workspaces + Turborepo:

```
pitwall/
├─ apps/
│  ├─ web/            # Next.js 14, App Router, TypeScript
│  └─ mobile/         # Expo, React Native, TypeScript
├─ packages/
│  ├─ shared-types/   # TS types shared by web + mobile (source of truth mirrors backend)
│  └─ ui/             # shared React components where practical
├─ backend/           # Python 3.12, FastAPI, uv-managed
└─ docs/              # ARCHITECTURE.md, brand.md, etc.
```

## Working style

- Make small, self-contained changes. One panel, one endpoint, one service at a time.
- After each change, state the exact command to run and what the user should see.
- Don't scaffold five files when one is enough. Don't add abstractions before they're needed.
- If a requirement is ambiguous or an API contract is unknown, ask — do not guess endpoints or fields.
- Never leave TODOs in place of working code without flagging them clearly.

## Data source rules (critical)

- OpenF1 is the only live source. Cache responses in Redis; never hammer the API from the client.
- FastF1 is for replay + circuit geometry only, never the live path.
- There is NO legal public source for onboard video. Do not scrape, proxy, or bypass any DRM.
  Keep video behind a swappable `StreamService` interface with a placeholder default.
- All timing/telemetry flows: source → backend service → Redis → WebSocket hub → client.
  Clients never call OpenF1 directly.

## Backend (Python / FastAPI)

- Python 3.12. Manage deps with `uv`. Type everything; run `mypy` clean.
- Pydantic v2 models are the source of truth for every payload. Mirror them in shared-types.
- One service per concern: TimingService, TelemetryService, StreamService. Inject dependencies.
- Use `httpx.AsyncClient` for OpenF1; never block the event loop.
- WebSocket messages are JSON with a discriminated `type` field, e.g. `{ "type": "timing.update", ... }`.
- Poll intervals live in config, not hardcoded. Be a good API citizen.
- Log with structured logging; no bare `print`.

## Web (Next.js / TypeScript)

- App Router, Server Components for shells, Client Components for live panels.
- State: Zustand stores, one slice per data domain (timing, telemetry, session).
- A single WebSocket client module owns the connection, reconnect/backoff, and dispatch.
  Panels subscribe to store slices — they do not open their own sockets.
- Panel grid uses react-grid-layout. Every panel is a self-contained component with its own
  loading + empty + error states.
- Tailwind for styling, tokens from brand.md. No inline magic colors.
- No `any`. Import payload types from `@pitwall/shared-types`.

## Mobile (Expo)

- Reuse `shared-types` and the WebSocket client logic where possible.
- Prioritize timing + track map; video is last and may be web-only initially.
- Test on a real device early — WebSocket + background behavior differs from web.

## Quality bar

- Every async path handles failure: API down, socket dropped, no live session.
- Show a real empty state when no session is live (it's not race day most of the time).
- Round all displayed numbers; never leak float artifacts to the UI.
- Accessibility: keyboard focus, reduced-motion respected, readable contrast in the dark theme.
- Don't commit secrets. Config via `.env`, documented in `.env.example`.

## When unsure

Ask before: changing the WebSocket message schema, adding a dependency, introducing a new service,
or touching anything related to video sourcing.
